So let's get started, shall we?

69
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

70
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Just the other night we hosted a couple of Weebelow's Den's at a troop meeting. They had come to visit us

71
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
And I got to say I'm never really sure what the best way to handle this is.

72
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
We've done all kinds of things. We've tried the PowerPoint presentation to the parents while the boys were off

73
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
doing some super special activity that the Scouts had planned for them and just absolutely fallen flat.

74
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
And I think this one went real well, And here's why It's kind of surprised us.

75
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
I mean, it was January, We were just coming back from the holidays

76
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
And you know we kind of threw it together. to tell you the truth,

77
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Let me tell you how it worked. The committee chairman talked to them for a bit about basic troop functions

78
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and administrative things and what it would be like to have a son in the troop. from that standpoint,

79
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
I had conferences and other things that I needed to do.

80
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
So while he was doing that I took care of business. and then I came in after about 20 minutes

81
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
and he introduced me to the parents and I said a brief hello and explained to them

82
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:49,000
that probably the best way for them to learn about our scout troop was to talk to our Scouts.

83
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
You know that I would be happy to talk to them for as long as they like,

84
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
but that I didn't think that that would paint as good a picture as our Scouts would.

85
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,000
One thing that we always do with Weeblows or visiting the troop

86
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
is. we send the Weeblows off with the Scouts to do whatever they're doing

87
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
and the parents come into another room and we spend some time talking to them

88
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and fielding questions and whatnot.

89
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,000
But just before I headed to that parents' room I asked a scout who joined the troop last year

90
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
if he could spare about 10 minutes.

91
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
I checked with his patrol leader make sure that was okay

92
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
And he said he'd be happy to do that and I asked him to follow me.

93
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
I didn't tell him what we were going to be doing

94
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
And I brought him into that parents' room and I introduced him to a group of parents

95
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and I explained that the scout had no idea why he was there

96
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
but that he'd be happy to talk to them

97
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,000
and answer any question they may have about what he's been up to for the past year.

98
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:55,000
And he looked at me a little confused but I reassured him. it wasn't a test,

99
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
It was just a chance for these parents to talk to him

100
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
and he found his feet pretty quick

101
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and I don't really know what they asked him or what he answered,

102
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:13,000
because I left the room to get a scout who had been in the troop for two or three years.

103
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
When I returned, I thanked the first year scout for his time

104
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and sent him back to his patrol and introduced the next scout,

105
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
explaining he'd been one of us for two or three years,

106
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And again left him in the room with the parents while he answered questions

107
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and I went to find our senior patrol leader.

108
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
I asked the senior patrol leader if he could spare a few minutes

109
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
and once again I really didn't tell him what he was going to be up to

110
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and I took him back to the parents' room,

111
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
sent our three-year veteran back to his patrol,

112
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
introduced the senior patrol leader

113
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
and the parents were able to ask him questions

114
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and I went out to get my next two speakers.

115
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
I would like to say that we had carefully planned this,

116
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:04,000
but it just was a happy accident that two of our alumni were visiting at the time.

117
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
I mean, it was, you know, we were in the middle of that big winter break for college,

118
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Found them in the troop meeting and I asked them if they had a few minutes

119
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and they followed me to the parents' room

120
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
where I thanked the senior patrol leader for his time

121
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and let him get back to his work,

122
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and I introduced our two alumni by saying something like you know.

123
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
so far. tonight you've heard from a scout who joined the troop last year,

124
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
one who's been around for three years,

125
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
and our senior patrol leader who's been around for five or six years-

126
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and I want to introduce you to two young men who were scouts in this troop.

127
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
They both became Eagle Scouts.

128
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
They're home for their winter break.

129
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Now you get a look at where your son could be headed if he joins our troop.

130
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Go ahead and ask any question you like

131
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and by the time they're done

132
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
you will have formed a pretty good picture of what a boy experiences

133
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
all the way through the years he can spend with our troop.

134
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Now those two fellows fielded questions for five or 10 minutes or so

135
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and I finished up with, you know, my five minutes of disjointed rambling.

136
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
I can edit the stuff I say on the podcast

137
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and it just makes me sound a little more intelligent that way.

138
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
I wish I could do that when I was speaking to a group, you know.

139
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
But we dismissed the parents meeting

140
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and one of the assistant scout masters had some pictures of our trip to Switzerland

141
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
to show them if they were interested in taking a look at it,

142
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and they could also go out and observe what was going on with the scouts

143
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
before we had our closing.

144
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
I was really happy with that Webelez visit.

145
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Now we've done the deal where we've brought scouts in to talk to parents before,

146
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
but it was a little more formal and this was a little less formal

147
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
and I think it worked pretty good

148
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and I think that the parents got more out of it too.

149
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
I'm not exactly sure what our scouts had planned for the Webelez

150
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
for that meeting.

151
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
They didn't turn themselves inside out,

152
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
but they knew that the Webelez were coming

153
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and they were ready for them

154
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
and took them through the meeting as they would in other patrol,

155
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
and I saw some happy faces at the end.

156
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
so I'll be looking forward to hearing how the whole visit was received

157
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
and see if we get some Webelez joined the troop out of it.

158
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Now that's one end of the spectrum-

159
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
bringing new boys into the troop-

160
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and the other end of the spectrum

161
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
is keeping our older scouts active and involved,

162
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
and it's always a perennial concern.

163
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
We bring our hands a lot over losing older scouts

164
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
and many troops do have a problem keeping them around.

165
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
I think a lot of times the standard response

166
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
is amping up the wow factor of the program

167
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and trying to keep these boys entertained,

168
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
but I've never really been fond of that approach.

169
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
I keep my hand in at our summer camp

170
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and work with the camp director and the program director a bit.

171
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
We are under constant pressure for the summer camp programs

172
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
to add yet another older boy program

173
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
to entice them back for another year,

174
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
because we think that they're not going to be interested

175
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
unless there's some big flashy, entertaining thing going on.

176
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
After watching this go on for 25 years or so,

177
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
these programs kind of come and go.

178
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
they don't really end up pushing the attendance numbers all that much

179
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and they drain a lot of resources.

180
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
I do believe older scouts want to have a great time

181
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
and do fun, exciting things,

182
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
but all scouts want to do that.

183
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Simply turning up the wow factor

184
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
doesn't really keep older scouts engaged

185
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and that's because they're looking for much more than just fun.

186
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
What really gets them interested,

187
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
What really keeps them around?

188
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
That's a pretty simple answer

189
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and it's kept our older scouts involved

190
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
well past the time you'd normally expect.

191
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
When we're rechartering each year.

192
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
our dropped scout survey usually includes

193
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
two or three older scouts because they aged out,

194
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
not because they've lost interest.

195
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Here's 10 things that we do to keep older scouts engaged.

196
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:04,000
Number one is our older scouts run the troop

197
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
with real independent responsibility

198
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
for the welfare of the troops program,

199
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
and I could just stop there

200
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and that would be enough, I think.

201
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
but that's tremendously important.

202
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
If they're not running things,

203
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
if they don't have that real independent responsibility,

204
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
they're not going to be interested for very long.

205
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Number two is our adult leaders

206
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
are taught to encourage and support

207
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
our scouts from a respectful distance.

208
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
We do our best to let them live their own lives

209
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
and not to interfere,

210
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and I think that's a pretty important aspect.

211
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Number three is we accept

212
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
that mistakes are inevitable

213
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and that they're not all punishable crimes.

214
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
We work hard to be encouraging and understanding.

215
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
when a boy steps out of line

216
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
or there's some kind of difficulty,

217
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
We try and give them as much latitude as possible

218
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
to make mistakes or errors of judgment,

219
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
without invoking the Nuremberg trial

220
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
or anything like that.

221
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
It's not a boys will be boys atmosphere,

222
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
not at all.

223
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
but we understand that they will

224
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
indeed make mistakes

225
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
and misjudgements

226
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
and we're prepared to deal with them

227
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
in, hopefully, a constructive and encouraging manner.

228
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Number four: we let these older scouts

229
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
be who they are,

230
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and what I mean by that is

231
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
sometimes that older scout

232
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
shows up with purple hair

233
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
or his choice of a hat

234
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
or some other kind of costume-

235
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
and when I say costume,

236
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
you know what I mean- right,

237
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
The stuff they wear to show

238
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
their rugged individualism

239
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
so that they look like

240
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
every other rugged individual.

241
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
I found that if we respond to all this

242
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
with nothing more than a slightly raised eyebrow

243
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and we accept

244
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
that this is a stage

245
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
that many boys go through,

246
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
we're not going to make a big giant deal out of it.

247
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Okay, so you want to do the purple hair thing

248
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
for a while? well,

249
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
I've seen that before.

250
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
you want to wear that crazy hat?

251
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
okay? well,

252
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
I was a boy once too and I probably look

253
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
just about as silly as you do now.

254
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
let them be themselves

255
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and don't make a big giant deal

256
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
over these things. that are just stages of growth.

257
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Number five: we accept that

258
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
not every older scout will be at every single

259
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
outing and meeting.

260
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
I guess what I'm saying is

261
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
is: we look at the glass as being half full.

262
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Beyond that, we also want them

263
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
to take part in other things- in the community,

264
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
in school and in their church and their family,

265
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and when they do,

266
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
they take what they've learned in scouting

267
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
out with them.

268
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Number six: we encourage a healthy

269
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
